{"name":"Buffalo Soldier","hex":"#95786c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"buffalo soldier","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An African-American soldier in the US Army, serving in one of a number of segregated units under white officers, in the period after the US Civil War up to the final racial integration of the US military, at the end of the Korean War."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buffalo_soldier"}],"entries":[{"title":"Buffalo Soldier","blocks":[{"text":"Buffalo Soldiers were United States Army regiments composed exclusively of Black American soldiers, formed during the 19th century to serve on the American frontier. On September 21, 1866, the 10th Cavalry Regiment was formed at Fort Leavenworth, Kansas. According to legend, during the exodus of the 1870s, more than 20,000 African Americans migrated to Kansas. They hoped their journey would take them far away from poverty from the South. Others escaped by being soldiers. They served in the segregated army units and fought in the Western Indian Wars from 1867 to 1896. According to legend, the men were called \"buffalo soldiers\" by the Apache and Cheyenne. The soldiers adopted the name as a sign of honor and respect. Units of Buffalo Soldiers answered the nation's call to arms mainly in the West, however, also in Cuba, the Philippines, Hawaii, and Mexico. They had a huge impact on the Reconstruction era."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Buffalo_soldiers1.jpg/330px-Buffalo_soldiers1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo_Soldier","wikidata":"Q1002108"},{"title":"Buffalo Soldier (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Buffalo Soldier\" is a reggae song written by Bob Marley and Noel \"King Sporty\" Williams and recorded by Jamaican band Bob Marley and the Wailers. It did not appear on record until the 1983 posthumous release of Confrontation, when it became one of Marley's best-known songs. The title and lyrics refer to the black US cavalry regiments, known as \"Buffalo Soldiers\", that fought in the Native American Wars after 1866. Marley linked their fight to a fight for survival and recasts it as a symbol of black resistance as a whole."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/dd/BuffaloSoldier%28Marley%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffalo_Soldier_(song)","wikidata":"Q141552"},{"title":"Soldier","blocks":[{"text":"A soldier is a person who is a member of an army. A soldier can be a conscripted or volunteer enlisted person, a non-commissioned officer, a warrant officer, or an officer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/2ID_Recon_Baghdad.jpg/330px-2ID_Recon_Baghdad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soldier","wikidata":"Q4991371"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/05462vBis.jpg/330px-05462vBis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/05462vBis.jpg/330px-05462vBis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Image taken in 1898 of the 9th U.S. Cavalry.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A05462vBis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Buffalo_Soldier_9th_Cav_Denver.jpg/500px-Buffalo_Soldier_9th_Cav_Denver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Buffalo_Soldier_9th_Cav_Denver.jpg/1280px-Buffalo_Soldier_9th_Cav_Denver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Buffalo_Soldier_9th_Cav_Denve